<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Apple's privacy manifest for the app target. Referenced by
  App.xcodeproj/project.pbxproj in the Resources build phase — a .xcprivacy
  sitting next to Info.plist that nothing copies is a file Apple never sees, and
  the mail it does not stop arrives the same day either way.

  WHY IT EXISTS: Apple's upload validator answers a missing required-reason API
  declaration with ITMS-91053 "Missing API declaration" mail after the
  TestFlight upload, and from spring 2024 that stopped being advisory. The
  required-reason APIs are a short list (file timestamps, system boot time,
  disk space, active keyboards, user defaults); an app that touches one and
  declares nothing gets the mail, and eventually the rejection.

  WHAT THIS APP TOUCHES, established by reading the code rather than by pasting
  a generic manifest:

    UserDefaults (NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryUserDefaults, reason CA92.1).
    @capgo/capacitor-social-login writes the Apple sign-in payload and its
    OAuth token store into UserDefaults.standard (AppleProvider.swift,
    OAuth2Provider.swift). That plugin is a local SPM path dependency of
    CapApp-SPM, so its code links into THIS binary and ships no .xcprivacy of
    its own — meaning nobody declares it if this file does not. CA92.1 is the
    right reason of the four: the keys are this app's own, read back by this
    app, never shared with an app group or another process.

  And what it does NOT touch, checked the same way: no filesystem plugin and no
  creation/modification-date reads (C617.1), no systemUptime (35F9.1), no
  volumeAvailableCapacity (E174.1), no activeInputModes (3EC4.1). The web layer
  keeps its own state in localStorage, which is WebKit's store and not a
  required-reason API. Capacitor's own two manifests declare empty arrays;
  RevenueCat's SPM package ships its own. If a plugin is added, grep its Swift
  sources for those five API families before assuming the list is unchanged.

  THE COLLECTED-DATA HALF MUST AGREE WITH APP STORE CONNECT. The answers in
  docs/ios.md §4 are the same three lines as below, and Play's are the same
  facts in Google's vocabulary (store/play/data-safety.csv, docs/PLAY.md) — one
  behaviour, three declarations, and a reviewer comparing any two of them
  should find the same app. Nothing here is used for tracking: there is no
  advertising identifier, no attribution SDK, and no third-party request during
  play at all, so NSPrivacyTracking is false and the domain list is empty.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>NSPrivacyTracking</key>
	<false/>
	<key>NSPrivacyTrackingDomains</key>
	<array/>
	<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypes</key>
	<array>
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPIType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryUserDefaults</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypeReasons</key>
			<array>
				<string>CA92.1</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
	</array>
	<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypes</key>
	<array>
		<!-- The leaderboard submission: a player-typed handle with a score,
		     level and line count, sent only when the player chooses to file a
		     score, and published on a board that is keyed to no identity. -->
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeOtherUserContent</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
			<array>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
		<!-- Entitlement state through RevenueCat. Linked, because for a
		     signed-in player it hangs off the app user ID below. -->
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurchaseHistory</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
			<true/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
			<array>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAnalytics</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
		<!-- `provider:sub` (src/lib/auth.ts's appUserIdFor), handed to
		     Purchases.logIn on every signed-in launch so a purchase survives a
		     reinstall and a second device. A durable user identifier, so it is
		     declared as one — not an advertising identifier, and not tracking.
		     A player who never signs in sends none of it. -->
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeUserID</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
			<true/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
			<array>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAnalytics</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
	</array>
</dict>
</plist>
